---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getFields, getSummary } from '../../lib/data';

const fields = getFields();
const summary = getSummary();
const maxPct = fields.length ? Math.max(...fields.map(f => f.pct)) : 0;
const totalWeight = fields.reduce((acc, f) => acc + f.weight, 0);

// Each field links to its section in the IPTC Photo Metadata Standard.
// We use a versionless URL — the iptc.org server redirects to the latest published spec.
const SPEC_BASE = 'https://iptc.org/std/photometadata/specification/IPTC-PhotoMetadata';
const SPEC_ANCHORS: Record<string, string> = {
  Creator: '#creator',
  CopyrightNotice: '#copyright-notice',
  CaptionAbstract: '#description',
  CreditLine: '#credit-line',
  Source: '#source-supply-chain',
  ObjectName: '#title',
  Keywords: '#keywords',
  DateCreated: '#date-created',
  LocationCreated: '#location-created',
  WebStatement: '#web-statement-of-rights',
  LicensorURL: '#licensor',
};
---
<Layout title="Per-field breakdown">
  <h1>Per-field breakdown</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The headline "% with any IPTC" hides a lot of detail. Below is the population rate of
    each IPTC field across the {summary.image_count.toLocaleString()} images analysed in
    the latest crawl. Field names link to their definitions in the
    <a href={SPEC_BASE} target="_blank" rel="noopener">IPTC Photo Metadata Standard</a>.
    The "scored" badge marks the four fields that contribute to a site's overall score
    (the "Four Cs" of news photo provenance); the others are tracked for context but
    don't affect the score — see <a href="/about/">the methodology</a> for why.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Field</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th class="num">Present</th>
        <th class="num">Total</th>
        <th class="num">Population</th>
        <th class="num">Score weight</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {fields.map(f => (
        <tr>
          <td>
            {SPEC_ANCHORS[f.field]
              ? <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}${SPEC_ANCHORS[f.field]}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>{f.field}</code></a>
              : <code>{f.field}</code>}
          </td>
          <td>
            {f.scored
              ? <span class="badge scored">scored</span>
              : <span class="badge tracked">tracked</span>}
          </td>
          <td class="num">{f.present.toLocaleString()}</td>
          <td class="num">{f.total.toLocaleString()}</td>
          <td class="num">
            {f.pct}%
            <span class="bar" aria-hidden="true">
              <span style={`width:${maxPct ? (100 * f.pct / maxPct) : 0}%`}></span>
            </span>
          </td>
          <td class="num">{f.scored ? `${f.weight_pct}%` : '—'}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
    <tfoot>
      <tr>
        <th>Total</th>
        <th></th>
        <th class="num"></th>
        <th class="num"></th>
        <th class="num"></th>
        <th class="num">{totalWeight ? '100%' : '—'}</th>
      </tr>
    </tfoot>
  </table>

  <p style="color:var(--iptc-muted);font-size:0.9rem;margin-top:1.5rem">
    Bars are scaled to the most populated field, not to 100%, so differences within the
    long tail stay visible. The score weight column reflects the per-field weights
    in <code>config/scoring.yaml</code>: the four scored fields are weighted 25% each.
    Tracked fields show "—" because their presence does not contribute to the score.
  </p>

  <h2>What this tells us</h2>
  <p>
    Even the best-populated field — <code>{fields[0]?.field}</code> at
    <strong>{fields[0]?.pct}%</strong> — appears on fewer than one in ten images we fetched.
    The fields most relevant to licensing (<code>WebStatement</code>, <code>LicensorURL</code>)
    are below 3%. The dataset as a whole tells a consistent story: publishers and CDNs are
    discarding metadata that the photographers and agencies almost certainly embedded
    upstream.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .bar {
    display: inline-block;
    vertical-align: middle;
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
    width: 100px;
    height: 6px;
    background: var(--iptc-bar-bg, #eaecef);
    border-radius: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bar > span {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--iptc-green, #6db33f);
  }
  .badge {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .badge.scored {
    background: var(--iptc-green, #6db33f);
    color: white;
  }
  .badge.tracked {
    background: var(--iptc-bar-bg, #eaecef);
    color: var(--iptc-muted, #6b7280);
  }
</style>
